WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.439
Pete: Tell me a little bit about the team
that you're building here and why an engineer

00:00:03.439 --> 00:00:06.690
would want to come and work at Tapad.

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:13.429
Well, that's an interesting question and of
course, it's not a hard one to answer actually.

00:00:13.429 --> 00:00:19.310
We're looking to build a team with really,
really skilled engineers, both on the technical

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:22.640
side, but also on the team dynamics side.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:31.519
We're looking for people that have already
a proven track record and that can come in,

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:37.870
get a problem, present it to them and then
start working on it with us and solving them.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:44.989
If you want to attract really good developers,
then you typically have to give them something

00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:46.359
that is interesting.

00:00:46.359 --> 00:00:51.710
And Scala has no doubt about it caused a lot
of interest in the Java community and also

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:59.710
in other language communities and being slightly
esoteric is a good thing, if you want to attract

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:00.710
people.

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:01.710
Pete: Yeah, that's an interesting strategy.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:08.460
And you have a unique perspective on repeatable
jobs versus jobs that require a lot of brain

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:09.460
power.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:10.460
Tell me about that.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:15.490
Dag: We have a slightly, I'm not sure if egalitarian
is the word here, but we don't hire first

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:18.380
rate and second rate programmers here, developers.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:21.530
We expect everybody to perform on the same
level.

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:25.689
And this also means that we're not going to
have people coming in that have to do all

00:01:25.689 --> 00:01:27.310
of the mundane tasks.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:32.020
We want to make sure that you have a team
that everybody has to do simple things every

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:37.189
now and then, but if the simple thing is something
that's repeatable then you should be automated

00:01:37.189 --> 00:01:39.300
instead of spending a time on that the next
time.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:45.090
So, we automate the simple and we spend our
brain power on solving the harder and more

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:46.110
interesting problems.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:51.969
There will always be a balance between the
really fun stuff, and then the more.

00:01:51.969 --> 00:01:56.091
Well, most things are fun actually, but we
can never guarantee that every single day

00:01:56.091 --> 00:01:57.189
will be super exciting.

00:01:57.189 --> 00:01:58.189
Pete: That's great.

00:01:58.189 --> 00:02:05.310
Dag: But I think when you put a lot of engineers,
really, really good ones into the same room,

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:09.030
they will always come up with really, really
good solutions, and they will be able to implement

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:16.400
them in a fraction of the time that a huge
team of less-skilled developers would.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.800
So, and that's our, kind of founding principal
that applies the whole organization not only

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.270
to the development engineering team, this
applies to the whole company.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:30.770
So, you will work with peers in product management
and in sales that are really awesome people.

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:36.160
Pete: So, anything else that's awesome about
Tapad that we missed that you want to talk

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:37.160
about?

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:38.880
Dag: I think we've covered a lot.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.950
I'm sure we have missed a lot.

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:48.670
I think the key takeaway here is the team
we're building and the fun we're having.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:50.670
Everybody's pulling in the same direction.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:51.670
Nobody is slacking.

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:55.560
Doesn't mean we're working people twenty-four/seven
here.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:03.470
Actually, that's one of my, one of my principles
is actually that I want people to have a sustained

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:04.780
pace all along.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:06.970
We don't burn the midnight oil every time.

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:14.640
We expect people to pull through if we're
in a tough spot, but now, the team is also

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:15.910
outside engineering.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:17.000
That's important.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.110
Everybody here is pleasant to work with.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:20.110
Yeah.

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:21.110
Great.

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:22.110
Awesome.

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:23.110
Well.

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:24.110
Thanks for chatting with us.

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:25.110
This has been really great.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:26.110
Thank you.

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:27.110
Yeah.

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:27.113
I appreciate it.

